\begin_layout Title
Do Voter ID Laws Reduce Voter Turnout for Democrats?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 5, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Ever since the 2008 presidential election, voter ID laws have become a hot
 button political issue, especially due to tight races in many swing states.
 While the debate has cooled since the last election, one can certainly
 expect that it will once again gain momentum as the struggle between Democrats
 and Republicans intensifies and the mid-term elections grow closer.
 As many as thirty-three states have proposed or already adopted laws requiring
 residents to show a government-issued photo ID in order to vote.
 Supporters say the laws are needed to combat voter fraud, while critics
 see them as a tactic to disenfrachise voters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

State law makers have claimed that new voter ID laws are meant to crack
 down on the problem of voter fraud.
 However, over a five year period, the Justice Department has only filed
 120 cases of voter fraud, most of them arising due to a misunderstanding,
 which have resulted in just 86 convictions.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

In 5-Year Effort, Scant Evidence of Voter Fraud.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 New York Times, 2007
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In addition, prior to the 2006 election, no state ever required a voter
 to produce a government-issued photo ID as a condition to voting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

According to a study from NYU's Brennan Center,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The Challenge of Obtaining Voter Identification.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Brennan Center for Justice, 2012
\end_layout

\end_inset

 it was estimated that about 11%, or about 20 million people, do not have
 government-issued voter IDs.
 Important to note, these figures only represent the population of people
 who are eligible to vote.
 The most likely people to lack valid photo ID are 18-20 year olds, those
 without college educations, minorities, and the poor.
 Recent studies have suggested that these demographics tend to vote for
 Democrats.
 So, while the amount of disenfrachised voters might not seem very high,
 it could make a difference in a close election.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, I would like to determine if strict voter ID laws actually reduce
 the percentage of votes Democrats receive during an election.
 Obviously, the state and federal governments keeps up-to-date voting records,
 so the data is readily available.
 I will analyze data obtained both before and after voter ID laws were enacted
 in respective states, and the observations will correspond to the fifty
 states.
 In addition, I will control for both state party majority, as well as state
 population demographics.
 The dependent variable will be continuous representing the percentage of
 votes to Democrats, while the independent variable of interest will be
 a discrete variable indicating whether a state has recently passed a voter
 ID law.
\end_layout

